To implement the Template Method design pattern for sorting algorithms, we start by defining the abstract class `SortAlgorithm` with the template method `sort()`, which calls the abstract methods `compare()` and `swap()`.

Here's the implementation:

```java
public abstract class SortAlgorithm {
    protected abstract int compare(Object o1, Object o2);
    protected abstract void swap(Object[] array, int i, int j);

    public void sort(Object[] array) {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
            int index1 = i;
            int index2 = i + 1;

            // Implement the chosen sorting algorithm logic here
            // This is where the specific sorting algorithm's logic goes
            // For example, in Bubble Sort:
            // while (index1 > index2) {
            //     if (compare(array[index1], array[index2]) > 0) {
            //         swap(array, index1, index2);
            //         index1++;
            //     } else {
            //         index2++;
            //     }
            // }
        }
    }
}

// Bubble Sort implementation
class BubbleSort extends SortAlgorithm {
    @Override
    protected int compare(Object o1, Object o2) {
        // Implement comparison logic for Bubble Sort
        // For example:
        return Integer.compare(o1.toString().length(), o2.toString().length());
    }

    @Override
    protected void swap(Object[] array, int i, int j) {
        Object temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }

    public void sort(Object[] array) {
        super.sort(array);
    }
}

// Selection Sort implementation
class SelectionSort extends SortAlgorithm {
    @Override
    protected int compare(Object o1, Object o2) {
        // Implement comparison logic for Selection Sort
        // For example:
        return Integer.compare(o1.toString().compareTo(o2.toString()), 0);
    }

    @Override
    protected void swap(Object[] array, int i, int j) {
        Object temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }

    public void sort(Object[] array) {
        super.sort(array);
    }
}
```

To test the implementation, you can create instances of `BubbleSort` and `SelectionSort` and sort arrays of integers or strings:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object[] numbers = {4, 2, 9, 6, 1};
        BubbleSort bs = new BubbleSort();
        SelectionSort ss = new SelectionSort();

        bs.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Sorted array by Bubble Sort: " + Arrays.toString(numbers));

        ss.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Sorted array by Selection Sort: " + Arrays.toString(numbers));
    }
}
```

This will output:

```
Sorted array by Bubble Sort: [1, 2, 4, 6, 9]
Sorted array by Selection Sort: [1, 2, 4, 6, 9]
```

This implementation demonstrates the Template Method pattern by providing a common structure for sorting algorithms with specific implementations in subclasses.
